    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe prove that it's the kernel of an evaluation map where you send all the X_s in the subset to 0 and everything else to itself. The hard direction is to check that if something is in the kernel then all its monomials had to have a bad X_s in, and the reason is that the monomials that survive evaluation give you a basis so in particular something linearly independent.</p>",
        "id": 606357607,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1782339161
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Just to record one thing: if you got lazy and used Aristotle (and it is very nice that you declare this openly) you cannot expect that people will review <em>its</em> code. We all try to help here but hoping to make people learn to write proper code themselves.</p>",
        "id": 606401254,
        "sender_full_name": "Filippo A. E. Nuccio",
        "timestamp": 1782366529
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/217875-Is-there-code-for-X.3F/topic/Ideal.2Espan.20of.20MvPolynomial.20generators.20is.20prime/near/606357607\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Maybe prove that it's the kernel of an evaluation map where you send all the X_s in the subset to 0 and everything else to itself.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's basically what I did, though I didn't try to use bases like you said, and thats most of the length is proving properties of the restriction map to show the kernel. I'll give your approach a try, thanks.</p>",
        "id": 606403795,
        "sender_full_name": "Vlad Tsyrklevich",
        "timestamp": 1782367854
